export default "## Why Blood Turns Brown\n\nFresh blood is red because hemoglobin (the oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells) is in its oxygenated state. When blood sits or travels slowly, hemoglobin loses oxygen and becomes methemoglobin, which is brown or dark in color. This is chemistry, not pathology.\n\nThe same mechanism that makes a cut look brown as it scabs makes vaginal discharge brown when the blood takes time to exit. Brown discharge before a period means the blood is old. Whether that is a concern depends entirely on context.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only and is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## The Normal Version: Slow Start of Menstruation\n\nThe most common reason for brown discharge before a period is that the uterine lining has begun shedding before menstrual flow is fully established. In the late luteal phase, as progesterone drops, the endometrium begins to break down at its edges. Small amounts of blood escape into the vaginal canal, move slowly, oxidize, and appear as brown discharge.\n\nWhen this happens one to two days before your period and the period begins promptly, it is a normal variation in how menstruation starts. Many people experience this pattern consistently cycle after cycle.\n\n## The Pattern Worth Paying Attention To\n\n### Brown Discharge That Starts Too Early\n\nIf brown discharge consistently begins four or more days before your expected period, the luteal phase may be shorter than optimal. The corpus luteum may be breaking down too early, causing progesterone to fall before the expected time. This early progesterone drop triggers premature shedding of the uterine lining.\n\nA short luteal phase (typically defined as fewer than 10 days between ovulation and menstruation) can affect fertility and indicates a hormonal pattern worth discussing with a clinician if you are trying to conceive or if the early spotting is bothersome.\n\n### Consistent Brown Discharge That Lasts Several Days\n\nBrown spotting that persists for four or more days before a period and never fully transitions to normal menstrual flow (or that transitions only briefly) may indicate a structural cause like uterine polyps. Polyps are benign growths of endometrial tissue that disrupt the normal lining architecture and can cause irregular, brownish bleeding.\n\nPolyps are generally not painful but can affect menstrual patterns and, in some cases, fertility. They are identified with transvaginal ultrasound and, if needed, treated with a minor outpatient procedure.\n\n### Brown Discharge After Sex\n\nBrown discharge appearing in the day or two following penetrative sex is typically from cervical irritation. The cervix is vascular and can produce a small amount of bleeding when stimulated, which oxidizes and appears brown. This is common and generally not concerning, particularly if it is light, brief, and repeatable under similar circumstances. Persistent cervical bleeding after sex, especially in larger amounts or with pain, should be evaluated.\n\n### Brown Discharge in the Middle of the Cycle\n\nBrown spotting that appears around mid-cycle (rather than in the days before your period) is often ovulation-related spotting, which is normal for a subset of people. However, mid-cycle brown discharge that is not associated with ovulation timing, or that occurs at irregular points in the cycle, may have other causes including cervical or uterine pathology and warrants clinical evaluation.\n\n## What to Log\n\nWhen you notice brown discharge, record:\n\n- **Date and cycle day** (count from last period start date)\n- **Amount** (trace, pantyliner, light)\n- **Duration** (how many days it persists)\n- **Whether it progressed** to full menstrual flow, and in how many days\n- **Any associated symptoms:** cramping, pressure, odor, pelvic pain\n- **Whether you had sex recently** (to identify post-coital cervical spotting as a possible cause)\n\nTrack this across two to three cycles. The pattern (how early it starts, how long it lasts, whether it is consistent cycle to cycle) contains the diagnostic information.\n\n## Color Differentiation: Brown vs. Other Discharge Colors\n\nBrown discharge before a period is usually not alarming. Be more attentive if you notice:\n\n- **Gray or greenish color with odor:** suggests bacterial vaginosis or STI rather than old blood\n- **Bright red mid-cycle:** suggests active bleeding rather than old blood and warrants evaluation\n- **Yellow with irritation or odor:** infection is more likely than normal cycle variation\n\nBrown alone, before an expected period, in the right timing window, is almost always old blood working its way out.\n\n## The Privacy Angle\n\nMenstrual cycle data (including spotting patterns, discharge observations, and cycle timing) is sensitive reproductive health information. Apps that store this data on servers can be subpoenaed in legal proceedings. In states where abortion is restricted, this data has become a tool in law enforcement investigations. Period app data is not covered by HIPAA.\n\nFlo Health's 2021 FTC action and $59.5 million class action settlement demonstrated that major apps have shared this data with ad networks and analytics partners without meaningful user consent. An on-device tracker stores nothing on company servers. There is no data to hand over, sell, or expose. That distinction matters for data as personal as reproductive cycle tracking.";
